   "about": "Tokuda recounts his trial, imprisonment, resistance to Japan’s wars, and release in 1945.",
   "summary": "The continuation describes courtroom resistance, Communist Party renunciations, and Tokuda’s transfer to Abashiri, where cold, forced labor, illness, and harsh discipline shaped prison life. Tokuda recalls warning fellow prisoners against the invasions of Manchuria and China, helping other inmates, and witnessing the failing health of Ichikawa and Kokuryo. He discusses reading and weather study, later transfers through Chiba, Kosuge, Toyotama, and Fuchu, and wartime deprivation and corruption. He says the prisoners planned the party’s postwar work before their release on 10 October 1945, then closes by linking prison reform to wider social reform.",
